#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# possible_zero_q

#% text
# `possible_zero_q(e)` gives true when e is probably zero.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# True when `simplify` gives 0; otherwise e is evaluated at three complex sample points for its symbols, first in machine numbers and then to 40 digits, and counts as zero when no value can be told from 0.

#% item
# `assumptions:` is passed to `simplify`. A true answer from the numerical test is a probability, not a proof.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
possible_zero_q((e + pi)^2 - e^2 - pi^2 - 2*e*pi)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
possible_zero_q(exp(pi) - pi^e)
#% output
#= false

#% cell
possible_zero_q(1/x + 1/y - (x + y)/(x*y))
#% output
#= true

#% cell
possible_zero_q(sqrt(x^2) - x)
#% output
#= false

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `simplify`, `n`
